    <note x="10" y="455" backgroundDark="0" fontSize="12" width="310" markdown="false" height="360" textColorDark="-1" textColorLight="-16777216" backgroundLight="0">1. Pick an experiment and a PT disruption and press setup (about 50 s). The experiment says who decides (a rule or an LLM), when, and the peak charge: $6 is the usual charge, $18 a higher one.
2. Press go: one morning plays until everyone has arrived at work, then go stops. Days 1 to 5 have no charge, days 6 to 10 have it.
3. To run day after day, switch keep-going? on before pressing go.
4. City-centre view zooms in on the gates and the queues.

Dots are commuters (colour = entry corridor, grey = works from home); green buses are commuters on public transport. Cars drive to the cordon, wait in red queues at the gates, then park at their workplace. Roads turn yellow, orange, then purple as traffic slows down. Purple motorways are in stop-and-go traffic (times from NZTA counts).</note>
