  <info>## WHAT IS IT?

The Cordon-Lite commuter model with every car driving on the real Auckland road network. Commuters live at their Cordon-Lite origins across Auckland and work in the city centre. Each one works in one building inside the cordon (SA3 "Auckland City Centre"), drawn at random once and kept for every day. Each morning a commuter drives (at a departure time it picks), takes public transport or works from home, as decided in the Python run. Each commuter has its own willingness to pay to drive into the city centre, and the share of commuters willing to pay falls exponentially as the charge rises; a commuter does not drive at a time when the charge is above its willingness to pay, unless it has a company car (the employer pays) or can neither take public transport nor work from home. On a replayed day a car leaves home, drives the TomTom major-road network through the morning traffic to the gate where its route enters the cordon, waits in its corridor's queue and drives on to its building.

## HOW IT WORKS

Road network. setup loads the TomTom major roads (`gis/tomtom_major_roads.shp`, converted from the prep GeoPackage by `python -m prep.build_netlogo_layers`) with the GIS extension and builds a node-link network the same way as the Python prep graph: a node at each segment end (NZTM rounded to 1 m), segments whose ends coincide dropped, and of parallel segments between the same two nodes the fastest kept (28,520 nodes, 30,846 roads). Only the giant component is kept, as the prep routes on it: 28,507 nodes and 30,835 roads, 1,106 nodes inside the cordon. The link weight is free-flow minutes (distance / speed limit). Nodes and links are hidden; the roads are drawn from the shapefile.

Routes. A commuter's gate is the prep gate of its origin (`../data/origins.csv`, `gate_id`; found through `origin_id` in the run's personas.csv, or the origin coordinates). Its car drives the free-flow shortest path from home to the outside end of the gate segment (a prefix of the prep path) and crosses the cordon at the gate point (`../data/gates.csv`). From the inside end it takes the shortest path to the cordon node nearest its building (in the largest connected set of cordon roads), with 60 minutes added to every road that has an end outside the cordon, so that a car that has passed its gate stays inside. On plain free-flow minutes about two thirds of these legs would leave the cordon and come back. A leg still leaves where the cordon's own roads do not connect: from the Wellesley Street ramp gate (about 100 to 160 m) and from Hopetoun Street (about 180 m). The car parks at that node (median 65 m from the building, up to about 780 m for buildings on the wharves, which the major-road network does not reach), and the commuter is drawn at the building on arrival. A synthetic folder whose agents are not prep origins (such as the equivalence check's test scenarios) queues each corridor at its corridor point instead; its homes can be kilometres from any road, so its cars start with a straight line and its speeds are not meaningful. A folder without plans files gets demo plans (every commuter drives, leaving between 06:45 and 08:00), and one without scenario.csv is charged from day 11.

Road traffic. A run folder made with road congestion on (road_sections.csv, agent_routes.csv, road_profile.csv and road_params.csv) splits each commuter's route to its gate into road sections. Each minute a section carries background traffic (from the AT/NZTA traffic counts, rising through the morning) plus the commuters' own cars that entered it in the last 15 minutes, each agent standing for about 20 cars (19,000 cars, 950 agents). The busier a section is for its capacity, the slower cars drive on it, so a car that leaves at 07:45 takes longer than one that leaves at 06:30, and fewer commuter cars ease the roads. This is the engine's procedure road-arrivals, the same as PyEngine, so every road delay equals the run's. Without these files (an older run folder, a synthetic folder) the roads are free-flow and the road delay is 0. The roads inside the cordon are never congested: the gate queue stands for that.

Motorway breakdown. Auckland's motorways run over capacity for hours every morning, and their flow breaks down into stop-and-go traffic. In a run made with motorway breakdown on (road_params.csv `mwy_breakdown` 1), a motorway section (a motorway road of the TomTom network by class or by name, such as the Harbour Bridge, signed 70 km/h or more, not a ramp: road_sections.csv `mwy` 1) is broken down inside its usual breakdown window (`bd_on`, `bd_off`) and at any other minute when today's traffic, with the commuters' cars, reaches its capacity. Motorway jam timing comes from NZTA traffic counts: on a motorway with count sites, each section takes the window, the capacity, the morning traffic and the capacity drop that the counts of the nearest count site on the same motorway show (road_sections.csv `profile_id`, `cap_drop`; road_profile.csv has one time profile per site), so each motorway breaks down at its own time. The usual windows are: the Harbour Bridge from about 06:15 to 09:30; the Northern Motorway between Albany and Tristram Avenue from about 06:45 until about 09:30, and the Northern Motorway around Northcote from about 06:45 until 11:00; the Southern Motorway between the city and Mt Wellington from between 06:45 and 07:15 until 11:00, and the Southern Motorway south of Mt Wellington from about 07:15 until about 09:45; the Northwestern from between 06:30 and 07:15 until about 09:45. The counts around Northcote and between the city and Mt Wellington show no recovery before 11:00. Motorways with no count site of their own (the Upper Harbour Motorway and the roads to the airport) keep the default window, from about 07:10 to 10:00 (six Upper Harbour stretches, whose capacity is raised to the minimum for their road class, jam a little later and clear at about 09:20 to 09:40). The counts at the Northwestern's city end and on the Southwestern show no breakdown, so the Northwestern's city end and the whole Southwestern break down only when today's traffic fills them. Broken-down flow loses capacity (the drop the counts show, 6% to 26%, else 10%) and moves at no more than 45% of the free-flow speed (45 km/h on a 100 km/h motorway). The usual windows come from the traffic counts, so fewer commuter cars on charged days cannot shorten them: they can only prevent the breakdowns that today's traffic starts. This is part of road-arrivals, the same as in PyEngine.

Clock. The minutes are those of the Cordon-Lite engine. Gate arrival = departure + free-flow minutes to the gate (agents.csv) + road delay (whole minutes). The queue is the engine's Vickrey point queue per corridor (procedure point-queues, the same FIFO order, carry and cap as PyEngine), so every gate-exit minute equals the run's. Arrival = gate exit + free-flow minutes from the gate. Before its gate a car follows its section times from road-arrivals: it covers each section's free-flow minutes of route in the minutes the traffic gives it, so it slows down on busy sections, and it reaches the gate point exactly at its gate arrival minute (its times are stretched by about a minute at most for that, as the engine counts whole minutes). Without road files, and after the gate, a car moves along its route at constant speed. Cars are drawn every 15 simulated seconds (one tick). A day lasts until the last commuter has arrived, then the next day can start. The workplace building therefore sets where a car parks, not when it arrives: the engine's gate-to-destination minutes refer to the single prep destination, not to the building. In a 950-agent R-clock run with road congestion and motorway breakdown the implied speeds to the gate (route length / (gate arrival - departure)) are a median of 42 km/h on days 6 and 25 (5-95%: 23-72 km/h); at free flow they would be a median of 78 km/h (5-95%: 50-95 km/h). From the gate to the parking node the median is 43 km/h (5-95%: 14-77 km/h; a few short engine legs give up to about 96 km/h). Queued cars are red and are drawn back along the approach road from the gate. Each agent stands for about 20 cars (agents_represented / agents: 19,000 / 950), so each takes up about 70 m of road (7 m per car, 2 lanes): the queue on the map is as long as the real queue. A queued car is drawn smaller than a moving one so that it fits its 70 m and the cars of a queue stand apart; in the Auckland view a whole queue is only a few patches long, so its cars still overlap there.

Road colours. The road sections on the commuters' routes are drawn a little wider than other roads and coloured by how fast their traffic moves at the last 5-minute mark, as a share of the free-flow speed (the engine's speed for a car entering the section at that minute): 80% or more keeps the grey of the road, 60-80% (slow) is light yellow, 40-60% (heavy) orange and below 40% (jammed) purple. A motorway section in broken-down flow is purple whatever its speed: it is in stop-and-go traffic. The slower the traffic, the wider the line. Red stays for queued cars and green for public transport, and the three road colours differ from the colours of the corridors' cars. The segments of each section come from `../data/section_segments.csv` (python -m prep.build_roads). Every 5 minutes only the sections whose colour changed are redrawn, at most 60 per tick, so when many roads change at once (about 07:25, 08:05, 08:45 and 09:15 to 09:20) the map catches up over a few simulated minutes; switching the view redraws the whole map at once.

Level of service. Below the map, the LoS monitors and plots grade the road sections on the commuters' routes at the last 5-minute mark, as the Python run does (cordonlite/roads.py): by V/C, today's traffic divided by capacity, in the HCM 1994 bands that SSC2026 used (E from 0.90 on motorways and 0.82 on other roads, F from 1.0; a broken-down motorway is F), and by speed (E or F at 40% of free-flow speed or less). "LoS E/F" is the share of traffic (vehicle-km) at E or F, and the mean V/C, weighted the same way, is shown beside it in monitors and in the plot under the LoS plot. The two plots draw one line for each road group (SH1, SH16, SH20, North Shore, New Lynn, Green Lane and all other roads), while the monitors show motorways and other roads as a whole. Read the two together, because the V/C grade saturates. The capacity of the other roads is a scale fitted to the TomTom congestion index (about 0.077 times the daily traffic count), not a lane capacity, so from 07:00 about 99% of their traffic is at E or F. On motorways about 76% of the vehicle-km is inside the usual breakdown windows, which are graded F whatever the flow.

PT riders are drawn as green buses (breed bus-riders, one per commuter on public transport that day): they leave home at their PT departure minute and appear at their building at their PT arrival minute (outcomes.csv of the run; without it they are at work all day). WFH commuters work from home: they stay at home all morning.

Workplaces. One building per commuter, drawn uniformly from the 1,484 building points inside the cordon (`gis/cbd_buildings.csv`, one point inside each footprint) with a fixed seed, in agent_id order, so a replay always shows the same workplaces.

## HOW TO USE IT

1. Pick an experiment and a PT disruption (none, day 3 or day 8) and press setup (about 50 s).
2. Press go. go plays the days of the run from day 1 to its last (10 days: the first five without the charge, the last five with it, from charge_from_day in its scenario.csv). One morning plays until everyone has arrived at work, and go stops. One tick is 15 simulated seconds.
3. To run day after day, switch keep-going? on before pressing go. go stops after day 10, the last day of the run.
4. skip to end of day jumps to the end of the morning without animation. Auckland view and City-centre view switch the map; the City-centre view shows the gates and their queues best. The NetLogo speed slider sets the pace.

The experiments in the experiment chooser: four ways the commuters decide, each at two peak charges. Who decides is a rule or an LLM. When: "reacts to events" means a commuter keeps its plan and thinks again only when something happens to it, such as the charge starting (day 6), a late arrival, a public transport disruption or a car trip that took much longer than expected (R-clock and L-clock in the Python runs); "decides every day" means every commuter decides afresh every day (R-daily and L-daily). The $6 experiments have the usual peak charge of NZ$6 and the $18 experiments NZ$18 instead, both charged from day 6: each run has 10 days, the first five without the charge and the last five with it (seed 1). Compare charges on the same days of each experiment, such as days 6 to 10. The PT disruption chooser adds two disruptions, on day 3 (before the charge) and on day 8 (with it): on each of these days the public transport time on one corridor (South-east, Alten Road and Parnell Rise: the corridor with the most commuters who can take public transport) doubles; it is announced that morning, and commuters who use public transport there think again that day and the next. Neither falls on the day the charge starts (day 6); "none" has no disruption. The commuters' behaviour was calibrated with the NZ$6 charge. An experiment whose run is not on this computer must first be made with python -m cordonlite.run (setup says so).

The monitors: "traffic delay (min)" is the mean delay on the roads of today's cars that have reached their gate so far; "mean queue wait (min)" is today's mean wait at the gates. The LoS E/F and mean V/C monitors and plots show how close the roads are to capacity (Level of service above).

## THINGS TO NOTICE

There are two kinds of congestion: traffic on the roads into the city centre and the queue at each cordon gate. The motorways go first: the Harbour Bridge turns purple (stop-and-go) at about 06:15, the rest of the Northern Motorway at about 06:45, the Southern Motorway between 06:45 and 07:15, most of the Northwestern between 06:30 and 07:15 and the motorways without a count site of their own (the Upper Harbour Motorway and the roads to the airport) at about 07:10, so by about 07:15 most motorways on the commuters' routes are purple. The Northwestern's city end and the whole Southwestern have no usual window: parts of them turn purple only when today's traffic fills them, mostly between about 07:40 and 08:30. The Harbour Bridge and the Northern Motorway between Albany and Tristram Avenue clear at about 09:30, the Northwestern and the Southern Motorway south of Mt Wellington at about 09:45, the motorways without a count site of their own at about 10:00, while the Northern Motorway around Northcote and the Southern Motorway between the city and Mt Wellington stay purple after 10:00 (their counts show no recovery before 11:00). On the other roads, at 07:30 most of the commuters' roads are yellow (slow), from about 08:05 most are orange (heavy) and the busiest approaches purple (jammed), at 09:00 most are yellow again and by 09:45 most are grey. Cars leaving in those hours slow down on the way, most of all on the motorways, and the "traffic delay (min)" monitor climbs. Watch the red queues grow behind the gates around 08:00 and the "waiting" counts in the City-centre view, before and after the charge starts; on charged days fewer cars drive in the peak, fewer motorways break down early, fewer roads turn orange or purple and the traffic delay is lower. The plots use hours on the x-axis (7.5 = 07:30). On charged days the entries plot shows the charge, scaled so that its peak equals the highest bar.

## PYTHON ENGINE

This is also the engine Python drives headless (NetLogoEngine, `python -m cordonlite.run --engine netlogo`): it calls setup-from-dir, set-clock and run-day, which load no map and write outcomes_dayNN.csv, profile_dayNN.csv and road_dayNN.csv into the run folder. Watching a run in the GUI writes nothing.

